---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **도착하나, 번지나.** (`/arrival`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **공개와 차트를 안 섞는다.** 우리가 본 것은 top10 에 뜬 주이지 넷플릭스가 언제 어디에
 *    올렸는지가 아니다. 같은 날 전 세계에 올려도 어떤 시장은 몇 주 뒤에야 뜬다.
 * ⛔ 「입소문」이라 안 쓴다. 나라 수가 늘었다는 것만 봤다.
 * ⛔ 스무 나라 미만은 안 센다 — 세 나라에서 둘이 첫 주면 67% 라 뜻이 없다.
 * ⛔ 줄세우지 않는다. 느린 쪽·빠른 쪽을 **함께** 낸다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-arrival.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-arrival.json';

const TITLE = 'Do Korean titles spread across the world, or land on it?';
const 번짐 = data.bands[0];
const 도착 = data.bands[3];
const DESC =
  `Among ${data.titles} Korean titles that reached ${data.minCountries} or more countries on Netflix's `
  + `weekly lists, the first week already accounts for a median ${data.medianFirstWeekSharePc}% of the `
  + `countries they ever reach — but ${번짐.titles} of them started under 25% and ${도착.titles} arrived `
  + 'nearly everywhere at once.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/arrival">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A distributor plans differently depending on the answer. If titles spread, the first market is a
    decision. If they land, every market has to be ready in the same week.
    Of the {data.titlesAll} Korean titles that charted anywhere across {data.weekCount} weeks, we took
    the {data.titles} that reached {data.minCountries} countries or more and measured how much of that
    footprint was already there in week one.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The median title is already in {data.medianFirstWeekCountries} countries in its first week</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Median</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Countries reached in the end</td><td class="tot">{data.medianCountries}</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Countries in the first week</td><td class="tot">{data.medianFirstWeekCountries}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Share of the footprint already there</td><td class="tot">{data.medianFirstWeekSharePc}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>We asked the wrong question first.</b> The original plan was to find the gateway market — the
      country a travelling title shows up in before the others. There is no gateway: the middle title
      appears in {data.medianFirstWeekCountries} countries in the same week. So the question became
      how much of the footprint that first week is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>And it is not one pattern but two</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Share of countries reached in week one</th><th>Titles</th><th>Share</th>
            <th>Median countries</th><th>Films</th><th>Series</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.shape === 'spread' || b.shape === 'landed' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.titles}</td>
              <td>{b.sharePc}%</td>
              <td>{b.medianCountries}</td>
              <td>{b.films}</td>
              <td>{b.series}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The two ends are almost the same size — {번짐.titles} titles built their footprint over weeks,
      {' '}{도착.titles} had it on day one. Averaging them produces a title that does not exist.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The ones that built it week by week</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Countries</th><th>In week one</th><th>Share</th><th>First week</th><th>Started in</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.slowest.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm"><i>{t.title}</i></td>
              <td class="tot">{t.countries}</td><td>{t.firstWeekCountries}</td>
              <td>{t.firstWeekSharePc}%</td><td class="nm">{t.firstWeek}</td>
              <td class="nm">{t.startedIn.join(', ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The ones that were everywhere at once</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Countries</th><th>In week one</th><th>Share</th><th>First week</th><th>Format</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.fastest.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm"><i>{t.title}</i></td>
              <td class="tot">{t.countries}</td><td>{t.firstWeekCountries}</td>
              <td>{t.firstWeekSharePc}%</td><td class="nm">{t.firstWeek}</td>
              <td class="nm">{t.film ? 'Film' : 'Series'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Films land more often than series — a median {data.byFormat.film.medianFirstWeekSharePc}% of the
      footprint in week one across {data.byFormat.film.titles} films, against
      {' '}{data.byFormat.series.medianFirstWeekSharePc}% across {data.byFormat.series.titles} series.
      A film has one week to be seen; a series can pick up a market in its third.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/arrival" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>This is not a release schedule.</b> {data.notRelease}</li>
      <li>
        <b>We do not say why a title spread.</b> A country can be added because more people started
        watching or because the titles above it fell away. Nothing here separates those.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Titles below {data.minCountries} countries are excluded</b> from the shares, because a
        first-week share computed on three countries carries no information.
      </li>
      <li><b>Russia is excluded</b> from every figure on this site.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/home-abroad">Does charting at home predict travelling?</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/catalogue-depth">All markets side by side</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
